///
/// lypning is a drop-in for whatever `python3` is on the host's `$PATH`
/// (`engines::find_cpython`), and `pyproject.toml` says `requires-python =
/// ">=3.9"` — so the set of interpreters this binary must agree with spans at
/// least 3.9 through 3.13, plus every release made after it was built. A table
/// that hardcodes one version's wording is therefore a table that is WRONG on
/// every other one, silently, at exit 0 or exit 1, through
/// `except … as e: print(e)`.
///
/// Measured on 3.9.23 / 3.10.20 / 3.11.15 / 3.12.3 / 3.13.12 on 2026-09-12.
/// Three ways to get this wrong were considered and two rejected:
///
///   * **Learn the host's version at runtime.** That means spawning
///     `python3 -V` — ~11 ms against this binary's ~0.64 ms whole startup, on
///     every run, to decide a string only a dying program ever reads.
///   * **Bake the version in at build time.** A wheel's crate is built once and
///     the `python3` it answers for can be replaced afterwards, so the baked
///     number goes stale in silence — which is invariant 1's "converts a loud
///     failure into a silent one", exactly.
///   * **Refuse** — this. It is right on every version, including the ones
///     released after this binary, it needs no table to be kept current, and it
///     costs one CPython spawn on a program that was already about to die with
///     no useful output. `CLAUDE.md`: a refusal is never a bug and a wrong
///     answer always is.
